; Redefinition — editing live code with a safety net
; Body edits late-bind silently; signature edits recompile every dependent
; and report exactly which world each symbol is in.

; A small call chain. Annotated params pin concrete signatures:

(defn price [:Int n] (* n 3))
(defn total [:Int n] (price (+ n 1)))
(total 9)

; A body-only edit keeps the signature. No ceremony, no recompiles —
; total picks up the new price body at its next call (late binding):

(defn price [:Int n] (* n 4))
(total 9)

; Now a chain where the edit will change a signature. g's body follows f
; wherever it goes; k pins its argument to Int:

(defn f [:Int x] (+ x 1))
(defn g [y] (f y))
(defn k [:Int x] (f (* x 2)))

; Redefine f to take a String. The session recompiles every dependent and
; reports the outcome: g rode along into the new world; k no longer
; typechecks, so it is marked broken — never silently wrong:

(defn f [:String s] (str s "!"))

; A broken symbol is not gone. Its definition survives, and every lookup
; explains what broke it — /info shows the provenance and the source:

/info k
/sig k
k

; Calling a broken symbol fails loud, with the same provenance.
; (The Error:-wrapper around the trap is a known presentation defect —
; repl/spec.md §18.5 specifies the bare runtime-error line.)

(k 3)

; The session shrugs it off: everything else still works, and g really
; is in the new world now:

(total 2)
/sig g

; Recovery, direction one: put f back. k is a static caller, so it
; rejoins the transaction and recompiles — good as new, zero residue:

(defn f [:Int x] (+ x 1))
(k 3)
k

; Recovery, direction two: fix the broken symbol itself. Break a fresh
; pair the same way:

(defn fee [:Int n] (* n 10))
(defn invoice [:Int n] (fee (+ n 2)))
(defn fee [:String tier] (str tier "-rate"))

; This time, redefine the broken caller to match fee's new signature:

(defn invoice [:String tier] (fee (str tier "+")))
(invoice "gold")
invoice

; And when a signature change has no compiled dependents, there is no
; report at all — as quiet as a body edit:

(defn solo [:Int n] (+ n 1))
(defn solo [:String s] (str s "?"))

; The safety net reaches unannotated code too. id generalizes to (Fn [a] a);
; g monomorphizes it at Int. Redefining id would strand g's compiled copy on
; the old body — a silent split world. Instead the session recompiles g at
; end of turn, silently: no report, because nothing is left stale.

(defn id [x] x)
(defn g [x] (id (+ x 1)))
(g 1)

; Redefine id. g's next call sees the new definition — 2 becomes 102:

(defn id [x] (+ x 100))
(g 1)

; Redefinition reaches the DISPATCH layer too. An impl is a body you can edit:
; re-enter it and subsequent dispatch follows the new bodies.

(deftype Box (MkBox [:Int w :Int h]))
(deftrait Sizeable (size [x] Int) (tag [x] Int))
(impl Sizeable Box (defn size [b] (match b [(MkBox w h) (* w h)])) (defn tag [b] 7))
(size (MkBox 3 4))

; Re-impl the pair with a new size body — same signature, so this is a body edit
; like any other, and the next call sees it:

(impl Sizeable Box (defn size [b] (match b [(MkBox w h) (+ w h)])) (defn tag [b] 7))
(size (MkBox 3 4))

; The truthfulness payoff: introspection lists ONE impl of Sizeable for Box, not
; one entry per impl form typed. The session reports what dispatches today, not
; the history of how it got there:

/info Sizeable

; A re-impl REPLACES — it is the whole implementation, not a patch. Omit a
; required method and it is rejected at the conformance seam, named:

(impl Sizeable Box (defn size [b] (match b [(MkBox w h) (- w h)])))

; Rejected means rejected: the previous impl is untouched and still dispatching.

(size (MkBox 3 4))
(tag (MkBox 3 4))

; Edit boldly: body edits are free, signature edits are accounted for,
; and nothing is ever silently wrong.
